## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design end-to-end performance tests for latency-sensitive, distributed systems that must handle burst traffic while maintaining strict SLAs.

## Key Concepts

- Tail latency (p99) measurement and SLA verification
- Backpressure implementation and queue depth management
- Autoscaling strategies (HPA/KEDA) and rapid scale-up behavior
- Distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry, Jaeger) for latency breakdown analysis
- Cold-start impact assessment in mixed serverless/Kubernetes environments

## Code Example

```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
import { Rate } from 'k6/metrics';

const errorRate = new Rate('errors');

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 20000 }, // Baseline
    { duration: '2m', target: 40000 }, // 2x burst
    { duration: '2m', target: 80000 }, // 4x stress test
  ],
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(99)<120'],
    errors: ['rate<0.005'],
  },
};

export default function () {
  const response = http.post('https://api.bidding-engine.com/bid', {
    auctionId: Math.random().toString(36),
    bidAmount: Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000),
    timestamp: Date.now(),
  });

  errorRate.add(!check(response, {
    'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200,
    'response time < 120ms': (r) => r.timings.duration < 120,
  }));

  sleep(0.01);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design comprehensive performance testing for a complex, multi-component real-time system. It tests understanding of end-to-end latency measurement across distributed components, resilience testing with external dependencies, and validation of autoscaling behavior under variable load conditions.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket connection scalability and lifecycle management
- Kafka throughput optimization, topic partitioning strategies, and consumer lag monitoring
- Redis data access patterns for presence tracking and rate limiting implementations
- Backpressure mechanisms and circuit breaker patterns for fault tolerance
- Autoscaling trigger configuration and failover strategies for external gateway dependencies

## Code Example
```javascript
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 30000 },
    { duration: '5m', target: 60000 },
    { duration: '2m', target: 100000 },
  ],
};

export default function () {
  const url = __ENV.WS_URL;
  const response = ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', () => {
      console.log('Connected');
    });

    socket.on('message', (message) => {
      const latency = Date.now() - JSON.parse(message).timestamp;
      check(latency, {
        'latency < 120ms': latency < 120,
      });
    });
  });

  check(response, { 'status is 101': (r) => r && r.status === 101 });
}
```

## Test Execution Strategy
Implement gradual load ramping to validate autoscaling triggers, inject fault scenarios to test circuit breaker functionality, and simulate external gateway outages to verify failover mechanisms. Monitor system-wide metrics to ensure performance targets are met under various conditions.

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to validate geospatial indexing performance, evaluate sharding strategy effectiveness, and analyze cross-service cache dynamics under extreme load scenarios typical of citywide events.

## Key Concepts
- MongoDB geospatial queries and 2dsphere index performance characteristics
- Sharding strategy optimization for geographic hotspot regions
- Redis cache warmth and hit rate optimization techniques
- End-to-end latency budget management and SLA validation
- Failure-mode testing methodologies: Redis outage scenarios, shard slowdown isolation

## Code Example
